#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# check-migration-replay — run `db:migrate` against a database that ALREADY
# EXISTS, and diff what comes out against the tracked `db/structure.sql`.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (K-1082). Every other gate in this repository builds its
# database FROM ZERO out of `db/structure.sql`: `demo:setup` is `db:drop
# db:create db:schema:load db:seed` in all eight demos, `production-smoke.sh`
# says so in as many words, and the only executable `db:migrate` anywhere else
# in the tree is `e2e/run.sh`'s — against a database `db:create`d on the same
# command line. So PRODUCTION IS THE ONLY PLACE WHERE `db:migrate` MEETS A
# PRE-EXISTING DATABASE, and a schema change the deploy path cannot deliver is
# invisible to 100% of the gates by construction.
#
# It has cost twice. K-436: the Manage-assistants page SELECTed governance
# columns a demo's structure.sql lacked -> HTTP 500 in production, CI green.
# K-1074: `add_column :users, :display_name` was APPENDED to a migration that
# had shipped a month earlier and was already recorded in every deployed
# `schema_migrations`, so the column reached structure.sql and every from-zero
# database and could never reach the running one -> four HTTP 500s on the live
# tudu box, CI green throughout. The defect stood four days in the TREE
# (`3a834cc0`..`86c89923`); how long the box has served those 500s is not
# knowable from here, and the outage is not over -- the repair only reaches a
# box on a redeploy. Both times the gates requested the very pages that were
# failing; the blind spot was never the HTML, it was the SCHEMA. "Curl the
# page" is the obvious and wrong fix.
#
# WHAT IT ASSERTS, in one sentence: for every vintage of a demo's schema that
# could still be running, replaying HEAD's migrations onto it must SUCCEED and
# must produce every object the tracked `db/structure.sql` declares.
#
#   1. A baseline `db/structure.sql` from history is loaded into a scratch
#      database — which is exactly how a box's database was born, whether by
#      `deploy/demo-reset.sh`'s `db:schema:load` or by migrating forward from
#      one. Its trailing `INSERT INTO "schema_migrations"` comes with it, so the
#      replay starts from the version ledger that vintage really had.
#   2. `bin/rails db:migrate` runs against it — the deploy's own command
#      (`deploy/CHECKLIST.md`, `deploy/README.md`), not an approximation.
#      A non-zero exit is a failure: that is a deploy aborting.
#   3. The resulting catalog is compared against a reference database loaded
#      from the demo's CURRENT `db/structure.sql`.
#
# WHY THE CATALOG AND NOT A TEXT DIFF OF THE DUMP. Two differences are expected
# and are not defects, and a byte-diff of `pg_dump` output cannot tell them from
# real ones. A replayed table carries its columns in the order the migrations
# added them, not the order a `CREATE TABLE` states them; and a replayed
# database's `schema_migrations` legitimately still holds versions whose files
# were deleted. Comparing named objects — columns with type/nullability/default,
# indexes, constraints, functions — drops both without hiding anything else.
#
# MISSING IS A FAILURE; A PLAIN EXTRA IS A NOTE; A HAZARD IS A FAILURE (T-132).
# The defect class is "the code SELECTs something the deployed database has not
# got", which is always an ABSENCE. A leftover object — a table whose creating
# migration was later retired, say — never returns HTTP 500, and it is on every
# real box forever, so failing on it would only teach people to switch the gate
# off. Plain extras are therefore printed; pass `--strict` to fail on them too.
#
# ONE KIND OF EXTRA IS NOT HARMLESS AND SINCE T-132 IT FAILS BY DEFAULT: a
# leftover column that is NOT NULL with no DEFAULT, ON A TABLE HEAD STILL
# DECLARES. Head's INSERTs do not name the column, so Postgres refuses every one
# of them — that is a live 500 waiting for a box of that vintage, the same
# severity as a MISSING object and the mirror of it (K-1086 shipped the DROP
# that repairs the worked example). The escape hatch is a DECLARATION, not a
# flag: ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS below, where every entry carries the ledger row that
# made it acceptable. A flag can be turned off for a run; a declaration has to
# be written down and reviewed.
#
# RETIRED IS NOT THE SAME AS LIVE, AND THE VERDICT NOW KNOWS THE DIFFERENCE
# (T-132, from K-1082's fix-ref). The HAZARD line used to read «every INSERT
# into that table is refused on a box of that vintage», which is literally true
# and connotatively far too strong for a table head has retired: `kiosk.actions`
# and `kiosk.action_log` were retired by K-828 and survive on every pre-retirement
# box forever, because retiring a migration does not drop what it made — and
# NOTHING at head inserts into them, so there is no INSERT to be refused.
# Measured on the 2026-08-11 window, six of the six distinct HAZARD objects are
# those two tables. Failing the build on that would teach exactly the scroll-past
# reflex that carries the next real hazard past a reader, so it is not a hazard
# here at all: it is reported as RETIRED, with the reason, and never fails
# without `--strict`.
#
# THE RETIRED/LIVE SPLIT IS DERIVED, NOT LISTED. A leftover's table is LIVE if
# head's own `db/structure.sql` declares it — which is exactly the reference
# catalogue this check has already built, so no list can go stale. If head's
# canonical schema has no such table, head cannot write to it: every gate,
# every box and every fresh install build from that file.
#
# IDENTITY IS COMPARED STRICTLY, DEFINITION IS COMPARED BY KIND, and the reason
# is measured rather than cautious. An object that exists under the same name in
# both databases can still RENDER differently, because `pg_get_constraintdef`
# prints the expression tree it was given: hoteling's
# `bookings_no_overlapping_room_nights` comes out as
# `ANY (ARRAY[('reserved'::character varying)::text, ...])` when Postgres
# re-parses the dump and as `ANY ((ARRAY['reserved'::character varying, ...])::text[])`
# when the migration's own DSL builds it — the same constraint, twice, and a
# strict text compare called it both MISSING and EXTRA in one breath. So a
# missing NAME always fails; a COLUMN whose type, nullability or default has
# changed also fails, because that is a real 500 (and information_schema
# normalises those three, so there is no rendering noise to trip over); an index,
# constraint or function that differs only in its rendered definition is a note.
#
# HOW FAR BACK. Replaying every structure.sql revision ever committed would be
# slow and would assert something untrue: nothing is running a schema older than
# the day the boxes were built. The window therefore starts at
# FLEET_SCHEMA_BASELINE below — the day the fleet was last rebuilt from scratch
# (`deploy/demo-reset.sh`, 2026-08-28 at head 2e4a767d) — and covers every
# distinct revision from the state on that date to now. WHEN THE FLEET IS REBUILT FROM SCRATCH (that is
# what `deploy/demo-reset.sh` does) MOVE THIS DATE FORWARD IN THE SAME CHANGE:
# it is the one fact this gate cannot measure for itself, and leaving it stale
# only costs time, while moving it early hides real defects.
#
# Usage:
#   bin/check-migration-replay                      # all demos
#   bin/check-migration-replay kiosk-demo-tudu      # one demo
#   bin/check-migration-replay --since 2026-08-20   # narrower window
#   bin/check-migration-replay --list               # show baselines, run nothing
#   bin/check-migration-replay --strict             # plain extras fail too
#
# Exits 0 clean, 1 on any aborted replay, missing object, or undeclared HAZARD.
# Needs Postgres and a
# psql >= 17 (the committed structure.sql files are PG17-format), plus each
# demo's bundle.

require "digest"
require "fileutils"
require "open3"
require "optparse"
require "shellwords"

ROOT = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)

# The reference fleet was last rebuilt from scratch on this date —
# deploy/demo-reset.sh reloaded every box from the head tree (2e4a767d) — so no
# database in service holds a schema older than the tree of that day. It was
# first provisioned 2026-08-11 (deploy/box-prep-2026-08-11.sh); the reset resets
# this window. See "HOW FAR BACK" above before changing it.
FLEET_SCHEMA_BASELINE = "2026-08-28"

# ── The leftovers that are ACCEPTED, and the row that made each one so (T-132)
#
# A leftover object is one the replay keeps and head's `db/structure.sql` does
# not declare. Most of them are the accepted consequence of a terminal decision:
# a table or a column was retired, the migration that made it was retired with
# it, and neither the DROP nor a rebuild ever reached a box that already had it.
# Nothing reads or writes them, and they are on those boxes forever.
#
# THEY ARE DECLARED HERE RATHER THAN MERELY TOLERATED, and that is the whole
# point of the entry (K-1086's fix-ref, carried forward as T-132). Tolerated,
# they were 139 note lines nobody read, `--strict` was unusable, and a NEW
# leftover arrived indistinguishable from them. Declared, each one names the
# LEDGER ROW that makes it acceptable, so the list cannot grow without somebody
# writing down why — and anything not on it is either a note the run prints or,
# for the NOT NULL-with-no-DEFAULT case on a live table, a red build.
#
# TWO KEY SHAPES:
#   * a bare `schema.table` accepts EVERY leftover object on that table — the
#     right scope when the whole table was retired, because its columns, its
#     indexes and its constraints all went with it and enumerating twenty-one of
#     them would be twenty-one places to keep in step;
#   * a full `kind|schema.table.name` accepts ONE object on a table head still
#     declares.
# Both are checked against the tree on every run: a table-scoped entry whose
# table head DECLARES again, or an object-scoped entry head declares again, is a
# stale declaration and fails. An entry that matched nothing in the window is
# NOT a failure — see the reach line in the summary for why.
ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS = {
  "kiosk.actions" => {
    row: "K-828",
    why: "the 0.3 action catalogue, retired whole. Its creating migration was retired with it, so a " \
         "box provisioned before the retirement keeps the table and no later migration drops it. " \
         "Nothing at head names it: it is absent from every demo's db/structure.sql, which is what " \
         "the retired/live split below reads.",
  },
  "kiosk.action_log" => {
    row: "K-828",
    why: "the 0.3 action catalogue's audit table, retired in the same decision and surviving for the " \
         "same reason. Five of its columns are NOT NULL with no DEFAULT, which is what used to be " \
         "reported as a HAZARD; there is no INSERT to refuse, because head has no such table.",
  },
  "column|kiosk.agents.notification_pubkey" => {
    row: "K-1290",
    why: "provisioned for a push channel nobody ever designed, and never wired: at removal it had zero " \
         "readers and zero writers tree-wide, every occurrence being this declaration or a copy of it in " \
         "a db/structure.sql. It is nullable, so unlike settlements.raw_jws it walls no INSERT and needs " \
         "no shipped DROP; the demos' migration history simply stops creating it and a box of an older " \
         "vintage loses it on the next deploy/demo-reset.sh. Object-scoped, because kiosk.agents is live.",
  },
  "column|kiosk.agents.kyc_attributes" => {
    row: "K-656",
    why: "the jsonb blob the KYC attributes were kept in before they became rows in kiosk.kyc_attributes. " \
         "The column is nullable, so it walls nothing; the TABLE is live, which is why this entry is " \
         "object-scoped rather than table-scoped.",
  },
}.freeze

# The `schema.table` an object identity belongs to. Identities are
# `schema.table.name` for columns, indexes and constraints; a function's is
# `schema.name(args)`, which has no table and simply never matches a table key.
def table_of(key)
  key.split("|", 2).last.to_s.split(".")[0, 2].join(".")
end

# Object-scoped first, then table-scoped. `accepted_key` is which DECLARATION
# excused the object — reported, so a reader can see which entry is doing the
# work rather than only that something did.
def accepted_key(key)
  return key if ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.key?(key)

  table = table_of(key)
  table if ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.key?(table)
end

def accepted_leftover(key)
  ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS[accepted_key(key)]
end

# ── A DECLARATION MUST NOT EXCUSE SOMETHING HEAD STILL CREATES (K-1293) ──────
#
# Every ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS entry says "an older box kept this and head does not
# make it any more". That sentence has a checkable half, and until this ran it
# was checked by nothing: if head's own DDL still EMITS the object, then what
# the replay found is not a leftover at all -- it is a live object that
# structure.sql fails to declare, which is a different and worse defect wearing
# the declaration as cover.
#
# MEASURED, and this is why the check exists rather than being a nicety: on
# 2026-09-05, with `notification_pubkey` planted back into schema_definitions.rb
# and the seven regenerated structure.sql left without it, `check-migration-
# replay` stayed at EXIT 0 -- and so did the kiosk-server suite (1552 examples),
# check-demo-copies, check-demo-derivations, check-prose-counts,
# check-publication-paths and check-derived-counts. Seven gates, seven greens,
# on a column the tree had just removed. The K-1290 declaration added hours
# earlier to make the removal SAFE is exactly what made the removal UNHELD.
#
# What this reads is the DDL SOURCE, one file, the single place these objects
# are emitted -- not a live migrate from empty, which would catch the same class
# and cost a database run per demo. Stated so the limit is not inferred: a
# declared object created somewhere OTHER than schema_definitions.rb would slip
# past this, and the fix for that is to keep emitting schema from one place.
SCHEMA_DDL = File.expand_path("../kiosk-server/lib/kiosk/server/schema_definitions.rb", __dir__)

def ddl_emits?(key, ddl)
  kind, ident = key.split("|", 2)
  ident ||= kind
  parts = ident.split(".")
  if key.start_with?("column|") && parts.length == 3
    # `kiosk.agents.notification_pubkey` -> the column name as the DDL writes it,
    # at the start of a line inside a CREATE TABLE body.
    ddl.match?(/^\s+#{Regexp.escape(parts[2])}\s+\S/)
  else
    # a table: `kiosk.actions` -> the unqualified name after CREATE TABLE
    ddl.match?(/CREATE TABLE[^\n]*\b#{Regexp.escape(parts.last)}\b/)
  end
end

def check_declarations_are_dead(ddl_path = SCHEMA_DDL)
  unless File.exist?(ddl_path)
    return ["ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS cannot be checked: no DDL source at #{ddl_path}"]
  end

  ddl = File.read(ddl_path)
  ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.keys.filter_map do |key|
    next unless ddl_emits?(key, ddl)

    "ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS declares #{key} (#{ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS[key][:row]}) as a leftover an older " \
      "box kept, but head's own DDL STILL EMITS IT -- so it is not a leftover, it is a live object " \
      "that db/structure.sql does not declare. Either remove it from the DDL (which is what the " \
      "declaring row said was done) or drop the declaration and fix structure.sql."
  end
end

# Named objects only — never row contents, never physical ordering. Each row is
# `kind|identity|definition`: the identity is what must EXIST, the definition is
# what it must look like. See "WHY THE CATALOG AND NOT A TEXT DIFF OF THE DUMP"
# and "IDENTITY IS COMPARED STRICTLY" above.
FINGERPRINT_SQL = <<~SQL
  SELECT 'column|'||table_schema||'.'||table_name||'.'||column_name
         ||'|'||data_type||' '||is_nullable||' '||coalesce(column_default,'-')
    FROM information_schema.columns
   WHERE table_schema NOT IN ('pg_catalog','information_schema')
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'index|'||schemaname||'.'||tablename||'.'||indexname||'|'||indexdef
    FROM pg_indexes
   WHERE schemaname NOT IN ('pg_catalog','information_schema')
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'constraint|'||n.nspname||'.'||rel.relname||'.'||con.conname||'|'||pg_get_constraintdef(con.oid)
    FROM pg_constraint con
    JOIN pg_class rel ON rel.oid = con.conrelid
    JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = rel.relnamespace
   WHERE n.nspname NOT IN ('pg_catalog','information_schema')
  UNION ALL
  SELECT 'function|'||n.nspname||'.'||p.proname||'('||pg_get_function_identity_arguments(p.oid)||')'
         ||'|'||md5(p.prosrc)
    FROM pg_proc p
    JOIN pg_namespace n ON n.oid = p.pronamespace
   WHERE n.nspname NOT IN ('pg_catalog','information_schema')
  ORDER BY 1;
SQL

options = { since: FLEET_SCHEMA_BASELINE, strict: false, list: false, verbose: false }
OptionParser.new do |o|
  o.banner = "Usage: bin/check-migration-replay [options] [demo ...]"
  o.on("--since DATE", "oldest schema vintage to replay (default #{FLEET_SCHEMA_BASELINE})") { options[:since] = _1 }
  o.on("--strict", "fail on objects the replay has that structure.sql has not") { options[:strict] = true }
  o.on("--list", "list the baselines that would be replayed, and stop") { options[:list] = true }
  o.on("-v", "--verbose", "print the migrate output of every replay") { options[:verbose] = true }
end.parse!

demos = ARGV.empty? ? Dir.glob("#{ROOT}/kiosk-demo-*").map { File.basename(_1) }.sort : ARGV
demos.each do |d|
  next if File.file?(File.join(ROOT, d, "db", "structure.sql"))

  warn "check-migration-replay: #{d} has no db/structure.sql"
  exit 1
end

# ─── plumbing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def run(*cmd, chdir: ROOT, env: {})
  out, status = Open3.capture2e(env, *cmd, chdir: chdir)
  [out, status.exitstatus]
end

def git(*args)
  out, code = run("git", *args)
  raise "git #{args.join(' ')} failed (#{code}):\n#{out}" unless code.zero?

  out
end

# -X so a developer's ~/.psqlrc (timing, pagers, \set ECHO) cannot get into the
# output this gate reads back — the same class of mistake as reading a result
# out of a stale file.
def psql(db, *args, stdin: nil)
  cmd = ["psql", "-X", "-q", "-At", "-v", "ON_ERROR_STOP=1", "-d", db, *args]
  if stdin
    out, status = Open3.capture2e(*cmd, "-f", "-", stdin_data: stdin)
    [out, status.exitstatus]
  else
    run(*cmd)
  end
end

def recreate_database(name)
  psql("postgres", "-c", %(DROP DATABASE IF EXISTS "#{name}"))
  out, code = psql("postgres", "-c", %(CREATE DATABASE "#{name}"))
  raise "could not create #{name}: #{out}" unless code.zero?
end

# "kind|identity|definition" -> { "kind|identity" => "definition" }, insertion
# order preserved so the report reads in catalog order.
def fingerprint(raw)
  raw.lines.map(&:chomp).reject(&:empty?).to_h do |line|
    kind, identity, definition = line.split("|", 3)
    ["#{kind}|#{identity}", definition.to_s]
  end
end

# An extra column that is NOT NULL with no DEFAULT is not a leftover, it is a
# wall: head's INSERT statements do not name it, so Postgres refuses them.
def hazard?(key, definition)
  key.start_with?("column|") && definition.end_with?(" NO -")
end

def database_url(name)
  host = ENV.fetch("PGHOST", "localhost")
  user = ENV["PGUSER"]
  pass = ENV["PGPASSWORD"]
  auth = user ? "#{user}#{pass ? ":#{pass}" : ''}@" : ""
  "postgres://#{auth}#{host}/#{name}"
end

# Every distinct db/structure.sql this demo has had since the baseline date,
# plus the one it had ON that date (the newest revision older than it) — that
# blob is the schema a box provisioned on the baseline day is running.
def baselines_for(demo, since)
  path = "#{demo}/db/structure.sql"
  log  = git("log", "--format=%H %cI", "--", path).lines.map { _1.split(" ", 2) }
  keep = []
  log.each do |sha, date|
    keep << sha
    break if date[0, 10] < since
  end
  keep.reverse.filter_map do |sha|
    blob = git("show", "#{sha}:#{path}")
    subject = git("log", "-1", "--format=%h %cs %s", sha).strip
    { sha: sha, label: subject, blob: blob, digest: Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(blob) }
  end.uniq { _1[:digest] }
end

# ─── the check ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

failures = []
notes    = []
replays  = 0
started  = Time.now

# Every `schema.table` head declares, accumulated across the demos replayed, and
# the number of leftovers each ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS entry actually excused. Both
# are reported: the first is what the retired/live split is derived from, the
# second is the only thing that distinguishes a live declaration from a dead one.
live_tables       = []
reference_objects = []
accepted_reach    = Hash.new(0)

# The declaration's own shape, checked here rather than trusted: an entry with
# no ledger row is the thing this list exists to prevent — a leftover accepted
# because somebody typed it in.
ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.each do |key, entry|
  failures << "ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS[#{key.inspect}] carries no `row:` — every accepted leftover names " \
              "the ledger row that made it acceptable, or the list is a place to hide things" \
    unless entry[:row].to_s.match?(/\A[KT]-\d+\z/)
  failures << "ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS[#{key.inspect}] carries no `why:` reason" if entry[:why].to_s.strip.empty?
end

demos.each do |demo|
  structure_path = File.join(ROOT, demo, "db", "structure.sql")
  tracked        = File.read(structure_path)
  slug           = demo.sub(/\Akiosk-demo-/, "")
  scratch        = "kiosk_mrp_#{slug}"
  reference_db   = "#{scratch}_ref"

  baselines = baselines_for(demo, options[:since])
  if options[:list]
    puts "#{demo}: #{baselines.length} baseline(s) since #{options[:since]}"
    baselines.each { puts "  #{_1[:label]}" }
    next
  end

  if baselines.empty?
    failures << "#{demo}: no db/structure.sql revision since #{options[:since]} — the window " \
                "excludes every vintage, so this demo is checking nothing"
    next
  end

  # The reference: the demo's CURRENT structure.sql, loaded. This is the
  # "db:schema:load from zero" every other gate uses, and the thing the replay
  # has to reproduce.
  recreate_database(reference_db)
  out, code = psql(reference_db, stdin: tracked)
  unless code.zero?
    failures << "#{demo}: the tracked db/structure.sql does not load (#{code}):\n#{out.lines.last(10).join}"
    next
  end
  fingerprint_out, code = psql(reference_db, stdin: FINGERPRINT_SQL)
  raise "#{demo}: reference fingerprint failed:\n#{fingerprint_out}" unless code.zero?

  reference = fingerprint(fingerprint_out)

  baselines.each do |baseline|
    replays += 1
    recreate_database(scratch)
    out, code = psql(scratch, stdin: baseline[:blob])
    unless code.zero?
      failures << "#{demo}: baseline #{baseline[:label]} does not load (#{code}):\n#{out.lines.last(10).join}"
      next
    end

    # db:migrate rewrites db/structure.sql on success; put the tracked bytes
    # back whatever happens, so a failed run cannot leave the tree edited.
    migrate_out, migrate_code =
      begin
        run("bin/rails", "db:migrate",
            chdir: File.join(ROOT, demo),
            env: { "DATABASE_URL" => database_url(scratch), "RAILS_ENV" => "development" })
      ensure
        File.write(structure_path, tracked)
      end

    puts migrate_out if options[:verbose]

    unless migrate_code.zero?
      tail = migrate_out.lines.reject { _1.start_with?("-- execute") }.last(8).join
      failures << "#{demo}: `db:migrate` ABORTED replaying onto #{baseline[:label]} " \
                  "(exit #{migrate_code}) — a deploy onto a box of that vintage stops here, and " \
                  "every later migration is unreachable:\n#{tail}"
      next
    end

    fingerprint_out, code = psql(scratch, stdin: FINGERPRINT_SQL)
    raise "#{demo}: replay fingerprint failed:\n#{fingerprint_out}" unless code.zero?

    replayed = fingerprint(fingerprint_out)

    missing  = reference.keys - replayed.keys
    extra    = replayed.keys - reference.keys
    reshaped = (reference.keys & replayed.keys).reject { reference[_1] == replayed[_1] }

    unless missing.empty?
      failures << "#{demo}: replaying onto #{baseline[:label]} leaves #{missing.length} object(s) " \
                  "that db/structure.sql declares — the deploy path CANNOT deliver these, so any " \
                  "code that reads them answers HTTP 500 on a box of that vintage:\n" +
                  missing.map { "      MISSING  #{_1}  (#{reference[_1]})\n" }.join
    end

    columns_reshaped = reshaped.select { _1.start_with?("column|") }
    unless columns_reshaped.empty?
      failures << "#{demo}: replaying onto #{baseline[:label]} produces #{columns_reshaped.length} " \
                  "column(s) whose shape is not the one db/structure.sql declares:\n" +
                  columns_reshaped.map { "      RESHAPED #{_1}\n        replay #{replayed[_1]}\n        tree   #{reference[_1]}\n" }.join
    end

    rendered = reshaped - columns_reshaped
    unless rendered.empty?
      notes << "#{demo}: #{baseline[:label]} — #{rendered.length} object(s) present under the same " \
               "name but rendered differently (usually the expression tree, not the meaning):\n" +
               rendered.map { "      RENDERED #{_1}\n        replay #{replayed[_1]}\n        tree   #{reference[_1]}\n" }.join
    end

    # RETIRED vs LIVE, read off the reference catalogue this run already built
    # rather than off a list: a table head still declares is one whose columns
    # are in `reference`. See "THE RETIRED/LIVE SPLIT IS DERIVED" above.
    live_tables.concat(reference.keys.filter_map { table_of(_1) if _1.start_with?("column|") })
    live_tables.uniq!
    reference_objects.concat(reference.keys)
    reference_objects.uniq!

    buckets = Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = [] }
    extra.each do |key|
      retired  = !live_tables.include?(table_of(key))
      declared = accepted_leftover(key)
      accepted_reach[accepted_key(key)] += 1 if declared
      verdict =
        if hazard?(key, replayed[key]) && !retired then :hazard
        elsif retired                              then :retired
        else                                            :leftover
        end
      buckets[[verdict, !declared.nil?]] << key
    end

    # (1) THE ONE FATAL EXTRA: a wall on a table head still writes to, undeclared.
    hazards = buckets[[:hazard, false]]
    unless hazards.empty?
      failures << "#{demo}: #{baseline[:label]} — #{hazards.length} leftover NOT NULL column(s) with " \
                  "no DEFAULT on a table head STILL DECLARES: head's INSERTs do not name them, so any " \
                  "INSERT into that table is refused on a box of that vintage. Ship a NEW migration " \
                  "that DROPs the column (K-1086 is the worked example, and the symmetry rule is in " \
                  "kiosk-server's schema_definitions.rb), or declare it in ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS with the " \
                  "ledger row that makes it acceptable:\n" +
                  hazards.map { "      HAZARD   #{_1}  (#{replayed[_1]})\n" }.join
    end

    # (2) The same wall, declared. Printed with its row so the declaration is
    #     visible on every run rather than only in the source.
    declared_hazards = buckets[[:hazard, true]]
    unless declared_hazards.empty?
      notes << "#{demo}: #{baseline[:label]} — #{declared_hazards.length} NOT NULL leftover(s) with no " \
               "DEFAULT, DECLARED in ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS:\n" +
               declared_hazards.map { "      DECLARED #{_1}  (#{accepted_leftover(_1)[:row]})\n" }.join
    end

    # (3) A leftover on a table head has retired. NOT a hazard however NOT NULL
    #     it is: there is no INSERT to refuse, because head has no such table.
    %w[declared undeclared].each do |state|
      keys = buckets[[:retired, state == "declared"]]
      next if keys.empty?

      tables  = keys.map { table_of(_1) }.uniq.sort
      message = "#{demo}: #{baseline[:label]} — #{keys.length} leftover object(s) on #{tables.length} " \
                "table(s) head has RETIRED (#{tables.join(', ')}): head's db/structure.sql does not " \
                "declare #{tables.length == 1 ? 'it' : 'them'} at all, so nothing inserts into " \
                "#{tables.length == 1 ? 'it' : 'them'} and there is no INSERT to be refused. Retiring " \
                "a migration does not drop what it made, so #{tables.length == 1 ? 'this table' : 'these tables'} " \
                "survive#{tables.length == 1 ? 's' : ''} on every box of that vintage forever" +
                (state == "declared" ? " (declared: #{keys.map { accepted_leftover(_1)[:row] }.uniq.join(', ')})" : "") +
                ":\n" + keys.map { "      RETIRED  #{_1}\n" }.join
      state == "declared" || !options[:strict] ? notes << message : failures << message
    end

    # (4) Everything else the replay keeps: an absence 500s, a leftover does not.
    %w[declared undeclared].each do |state|
      keys = buckets[[:leftover, state == "declared"]]
      next if keys.empty?

      message = "#{demo}: #{baseline[:label]} — #{keys.length} leftover object(s) the replay keeps and " \
                "structure.sql does not declare (an absence 500s, a leftover does not)" +
                (state == "declared" ? ", DECLARED in ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS" : "") + ":\n" +
                keys.map { state == "declared" ? "      DECLARED #{_1}  (#{accepted_leftover(_1)[:row]})\n" : "      EXTRA    #{_1}\n" }.join
      state == "declared" || !options[:strict] ? notes << message : failures << message
    end
  end
end

exit 0 if options[:list]

# A DECLARATION THAT NO LONGER DESCRIBES THE TREE. A table-scoped entry is a
# statement that head has retired the table; an object-scoped one that head no
# longer declares the object. Either coming BACK makes the entry a lie that
# silently accepts a live object, so it is checked against the same reference
# catalogues the split is derived from — never against a remembered list.
unless options[:list] || live_tables.empty?
  ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.each_key do |key|
    if key.include?("|")
      next unless reference_objects.include?(key)

      failures << "ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS declares #{key} a leftover, and head's db/structure.sql declares " \
                  "it again — the object is back, so the entry now excuses something live. Drop it."
    else
      next unless live_tables.include?(key)

      failures << "ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS declares the whole of #{key} retired, and head's db/structure.sql " \
                  "declares that table — every leftover on a LIVE table is now excused by this entry, " \
                  "including a NOT NULL wall. Drop it, or narrow it to the objects that are really gone."
    end
  end
end

elapsed = (Time.now - started).round(1)

unless notes.empty?
  puts "\ncheck-migration-replay NOTES (not failures):\n"
  notes.each { puts "  - #{_1}" }
end

# THE REACH OF THE DECLARATION, PRINTED EVERY RUN. An allow-list nobody reaches
# looks exactly like an allow-list that is holding something, and this one is
# reached ZERO times in the default window on purpose: the fleet was rebuilt on
# FLEET_SCHEMA_BASELINE, so no vintage in the window still carries the retired
# tables. That is a fact about the window, not about the entries, and widening
# the window with `--since` brings them back — which is the only way to see them
# work. It is a NOTE and not a failure for exactly that reason: making it fatal
# would demand the defect exist. (`--since 2026-08-11`, the pre-rebuild window,
# measured 2026-08-31: 22 distinct leftover objects, all three entries reached,
# zero undeclared.)
unless ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.empty? || options[:list]
  reached = ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.keys.count { accepted_reach[_1].positive? }
  detail  = ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.map { |k, e| "#{k} (#{e[:row]}) x#{accepted_reach[k]}" }.join(", ")
  puts "\ncheck-migration-replay: ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS — #{ACCEPTED_LEFTOVERS.size} declared, " \
       "#{reached} reached in this window [#{detail}]. Zero reached is expected while the window " \
       "starts after the last fleet rebuild; it is never a failure, because a declaration can only " \
       "be exercised by the defect it declares."
end

# K-1293: every declaration must still be DEAD at head. Appended to `failures`
# so it exits through the same path and prints with the same shape -- a check
# that reports on its own channel is one a reader learns to scroll past.
failures.concat(check_declarations_are_dead) unless options[:list]

if failures.empty?
  puts "check-migration-replay: OK — #{replays} replay(s) across #{demos.length} demo(s), " \
       "every schema vintage since #{options[:since]} migrates to the tracked structure.sql (#{elapsed}s)"
  exit 0
end

warn "\ncheck-migration-replay FAILED:\n"
failures.each { warn "  x #{_1}" }
warn <<~WHY

  A migration set that only works FROM ZERO is a migration set the deploy cannot
  deliver. The repair is a NEW migration file, never an edit to one that has
  already shipped: a shipped migration is recorded in every deployed
  `schema_migrations` and will never run again (T-103,
  MIGRATION-AND-CONFIG-UPGRADE-POLICY). If the replay ABORTED, the emitted DDL is
  not replay-safe — see Kiosk::Server::SchemaDefinitions.
WHY
exit 1
